{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-158261","model_call":37,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"china's accession to the wto\" (+ \"united states\" u.s. america) (+ \"permanent normal trade relations\" pntr exports growth investment))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"china's accession to the wto\" (+ \"united states\" u.s. america) (+ \"permanent normal trade relations\" pntr exports growth investment))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_61956436#5_74206996","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1700","rank":1,"snippet":"Every year between 1989 and 1999, legislation was introduced in Congress to disapprove the President's waiver. The legislation had sought to tie free trade with China to meeting certain human rights conditions that go beyond freedom of emigration. All such attempted legislation failed to pass. The requirement of an annual waiver was inconsistent with the rules of the World Trade Organization, and for the PRC to join the WTO, Congressional action was needed to grant permanent normal trade relations (<cover>PNTR) to China. This was accomplished in 2000 with the United States–China Relations Act of 2000, allowing China to join WTO in 2001. China's most favoured nation (MFN) status was made permanent on December 27, 2001. President Bill Clinton in 2000 pushed Congress to approve the U.S.-China trade agreement and China's accession to the WTO, </cover>saying that more trade with China would advance America's economic interests: \"\" , \"start_char\": 4566.000000 , \"title\": \"United States–China Relations Act of 2000 - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S.%E2%80%93China_Relations_Act_of_2000\" }"},"time":140},"model_calls":37,"document_judgments":23,"relevance_3":5,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
