{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-158261","model_call":72,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"allowing china\" wto (+ \"united states\" clinton congress) (+ growth exports trade))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"allowing china\" wto (+ \"united states\" clinton congress) (+ growth exports trade))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_46202363#5_88798334","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1721","rank":1,"snippet":"and firms, and in favor of multinational companies (MNCs) from the United States and other countries, and state- and privately-owned exporters in China. This has increased the global “race to the bottom” in wages and environmental quality and caused the closing of thousands of U.S. factories, decimating employment in a wide range of communities, states, and entire regions of the United States. False promises Proponents of China’s entry into the WTO frequently claimed that it would create jobs in the United States, increase U.S. exports, and improve the <cover>trade deficit with China. President Clinton claimed that the agreement allowing China into the WTO, </cover>which was negotiated during his administration, “creates a win-win result for both countries” (Clinton 2000, 9). He argued that exports to China “now support hundreds of thousands of American jobs” and that “these figures can grow substantially with the new access to the Chinese market the WTO agreement creates” (Clinton 2000, 10).\" , \"start_char\": 3110.000000 , \"title\": \"Costly Trade With China: Millions of U.S. jobs displaced with net job loss in every state | Economic Policy Institute\" , \"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_46202363#5_88798334","rank":1,"relevance":2}]},"time":90},"model_calls":72,"document_judgments":46,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
